  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b7/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_17.png",
  "plot_summary": "The FF wave goodbye to Ant-Man as he streaks away, thanks to Reed's makeshift \"launching platform.\"&nbsp; The next page recaps their encounter with Dr. Doom in the micro world from the previous issue.\n\nReed produces a radar device that's \"extra sensitive to human flesh covered by steel!\"&nbsp; While he's using it to search for Doom, the others begin searching in their own ways, including Ben, on the street where a woman says \"He's so grotesque that he's almost <i>beautiful</i>!\" (<i>what</i>?)&nbsp; They finally all give up.&nbsp; Doom seems to have been too clever to leave clues -- and Reed admits they'll have to wait for him to make the first move.\n\nThey prepare to \"continue with our normal social lives,\" then attempt to slip past the throng of fans in the lobby, heading out through the freight elevator where an old janitor fan insists on shaking their hands, but we quickly learn that it's Doom in disguise, and he places a tracker on each of their hands during the handshake.&nbsp; He returns to his \"hidden lab\" and sends out four \"lighter-than-air robots\" to tackle the FF individually.&nbsp; Each member of the team is shown around town being pestered by one of them, before all four return to HQ where Reed has a hunch where they came from.&nbsp; They discover the discs and get rid of them, but it's only the first phase of Doom's plan.\n\nHe considers his brain \"the equal of Reed Richards' himself\" but admits he doesn't understand other humans, but seeing the Thing be with Alicia shows him their weak spot -- even the Thing can be out among \"normal people\" while he hides away.&nbsp; He sends out a \"grappler ray\" to Alicia, which lifts her up through the air toward Doom, who we find is hidden high above in a cloud where his floating lab is.\n\nAlicia's floating in the air makes the front page of that evening's paper, and the Thing sees her.&nbsp; Doom's face appears in the air, claiming that he is \"still human enough to wish to <i>gloat</i> over your hapless plight!\"&nbsp; He threatens that if they don't leave him alone, he will unleash disaster on the city, and he has Alicia as his trump card in the end.&nbsp; Reed doesn't care -- he's going after him, but the Thing wants no part of it -- Alicia must be kept safe.\n\nDoom exalts as they begin fighting amongst themselves, and we learn that he doesn't need money but only wants a \"post in the President's cabinet.&nbsp; It is only fitting that a man of my ability have at least cabinet rank in the government!\" (see note below)&nbsp; We're given a brief scene in the Oval office, where we see only the back and top half of the President's head while they discuss the demands.&nbsp; It's clearly JFK, and he excuses himself saying that \"It's Caroline's bedtime!\"\n\nDoom retaliates for the snub by beginning a one-man war.&nbsp; Everywhere machines run amok, in factories, military operations, etc.&nbsp; \"Almost overnight, the mighty industrial complex of America seems to grind to a halt!\"&nbsp; We see the Russians rejoice before \"Comrade K\" tells him to cut it out - \"for all we know, he may strike <i>here</i> next!\"\n\nThe government is forced to plead for help from the FF.&nbsp; Reed has already been thinking about it.&nbsp; With his scanners, he's learned that the four robots from earlier were actually reading their atomic structures, and that now if they approach Doom's ship, he has their patterns to zero in on with his disintegrator rays.&nbsp; Reed realizes that the only one who can slip past that defense is the Thing, and only if he can become Ben Grimm, because that pattern has <i>not</i> been stored by Doom.&nbsp; After long hours, he comes up with a formula, which successfully transforms him back to human form, but for how long remains unknown.\n\nBen flies toward the floating lab in a \"silent, transparent, magnetically powered, one-man plastic bubble.\"&nbsp; He successfully gets past the rotating disintegrators!&nbsp; On board the ship, he changes back to the Thing and destroys the disintegrators.&nbsp; The rest of the team is now able to join, and they rush off in separate directions to face the various menaces we saw depicted on the cover.&nbsp; But of course, they get past them, causing Doom to exclaim \"That accursed foursome has more lives than a pack of <i>cats</i>!\"&nbsp; He unleashes the \"dread fury of my hurtling power spheres!\"&nbsp; He notes that he doesn't see the girl (referring to Sue), \"but she is unimportant!\"\n\nThe power spheres encase the other three, and they send them into another dimension -- Doom has won!&nbsp; But the three soon burst into the room, and it turns out that the Torch had cast three \"flame images\" -- Doom is unfazed -- he still has Alicia.&nbsp; But we learn then that Sue has gone off to find her, which is why she wasn't there.&nbsp; She switches places with Alicia and is ready for Doom when he goes to retrieve Alicia.&nbsp; Doom and Sue battle for over a page, and we find out that Sue knows judo, having been taught \"by one of the world's greatest experts:&nbsp; Reed Richards!\"\n\nTheir fight is interrupted by the arrival of the other three, and Doom realizes that retreat is the best course of action.&nbsp; He jumps out of the floating lab and plummets downward out of sight.\n\nReunions all around:&nbsp; Reed and Sue!&nbsp; Ben and Alicia!&nbsp; It's enough \"mush\" to nauseate anyone, and Johnny says \"I don't <i>blame</i> Doctor Doom for <i>jumpin'</i>!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "Some brief character beats, but story drift and gadget padding overwhelm them.\nBetter than last month, but it still feels like an obligation read.",
  "subtitle": "Doom Returns, the Lab Floats... Doom Still Doesn't.",
  "review_text": "Following directly on the heels of last month's encounter with Doom in the micro-world, this one feels almost like a very early second-parter, but Ant-Man excuses himself on the splash page, leaving the Fantastic Four to perform mop-up duty.\n\nThe book does its normal stall tactic in the early going, with page 2 taken up in recap, followed by a couple of pages of fruitless searching by Johnny, Ben and Sue, and it isn't until page 5 before Reed finally admits they'll have to wait for Doom to make the first move (though the scene where they have to navigate past a floor landing full of adoring fans is a bit much).\n\n[ASSET: right | DeepAffection | width=12%] This is not to say that the opening pages were without <i>any </i>lasting value.&nbsp; For instance, page 3 shows the Human Torch heading out to search the city, clearly pointing out that he actually has deep affection for Ben, unlike what their ongoing (and very outwardly) hostile actions show.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is very similar to the panel showing the reverse in the Human Torch half of [LINK:1963:ST_109_torch0|ST #109], where Ben's affection is highlighted, so this may be the first time we learn Johnny feels the same way.\n\nLikewise, there are moments where we begin to see the Thing leaning into his popularity, even pointing out in one panel that he doesn't need to be recognized due to his looks, as \"it's my lovable <i>personality</i> that shines out like a beacon!\"&nbsp; (And this is after the woman's comment on page 4 that \"He's so grotesque that he's almost <i>beautiful</i>!\" (which I found eye-opening -- I'm not entirely sure how <i>that</i> works!)\n\n[ASSET: left | IdentoDiscs | width=22%] But eventually, the secret plan of Dr. Doom has no choice but to move forward, and janitor-Doom secretly plants trackers on each of them for tracking purposes, sending out his \"lighter-than-air\" robots to menace each one in turn.\n[BR]\n[BR] (This <i>is</i> sort of the theme of the issue's cover, after all -- Doom manipulating a device while all four members of the FF are individually threatened by various obstacles -- though the ones actually shown on the cover come later in the story.)\n\nBut if you think about it, the robots themselves are essentially harmless and seemed designed to do nothing more than consume another three pages.&nbsp; By the bottom of page 8, Reed has figured out that Doom is behind all of it, and Doom himself is raising a fist saying that it's time for the next phase of his plan.&nbsp; None of this rises above perfunctory plot‑maintenance.\n\n[ASSET: right | EqualToReedRichards | width=10%] I was a little surprised when Doom stated on page 9 that he considers his brain \"the equal of Reed Richards' himself.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] He's always struck me as being one who considers others <i>lesser</i> than himself, <i>hardly</i> equal.\n[BR]\n[BR] In fact, page 23 of his debut issue ([LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5]) even has him say that his is \"the greatest scientific brain of all time.\"\n\nHis later capture of Alicia only serves to show that his \"hidden lab\" is inside a floating craft concealed by a cloud above the city.&nbsp; We're about halfway through the story at this point, and still... nothing much has happened.\n\nWe learn on page 11 that Doom seeks power above all else.&nbsp; He has all the money he needs, but he wants power, and somehow, he believes he will get that by demanding \"a post in the President's cabinet!\"\n[ASSET: center | ModestDemands | width=67%]\nNote that when Doom refers to \"the government\" -- it gives readers the impression that Doom sees the US government as \"the\" government, implying a domestic frame of reference.&nbsp; He's still maniacal enough to threaten war on the nation if his demands are not met, but that's beside the point.\n\nWith the world threatened, Alicia held captive, and Doom holding the power to disintegrate each member of the FF, Reed is forced to find a way to turn the Thing back to Ben Grimm long enough to slip past (and destroy) Doom's defenses so that they can slip aboard.&nbsp; This, at least, was a decent idea.\n\nBut of course, once on board the ship, Kirby is left free to depict nothing other than a series of death traps, and all this does is suck up yet another handful of pages.\n\n[ASSET: right | CannotSeeSue | width=13%] Doom states on page 18 that, \"I do not see the girl, but she is unimportant!\"&nbsp; And while I give credit to Stan and Jack for making deliberate attempts to get Sue more involved in the battles, I do still have to wonder:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Why would someone as intelligent as Dr. Doom think it odd that he could not \"see\" an opponent whose name is literally \"The Invisible Girl?\"&nbsp; Isn't that exactly what we'd <i>expect</i> of one with her powers?]&nbsp; At least this allows Sue free to find Alicia and get her out of harm's way.\n\nMore to the point though, the continual attempts to get Sue involved come across as genuinely reaching on the part of the co-creators (they devote the entirety of pages 20 and 21 to this).&nbsp; While it's admittedly handy to be able to hide from people by becoming invisible, Sue quite literally has <i>no offensive capability</i> at all (we saw this made painfully obvious late in 1962 over in [LINK:1962:ST_105_torch0|ST #105]).\n\nThe fact that her contributions had to be explicitly spelled out to readers as recently as [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11] only serves to underscore how limited she is (other than knowing some judo that Reed taught her).&nbsp; Something will need to change if she's to contribute meaningfully in future adventures.\n\nOh, and while I'm on the subject of judo, I think it's fair to wonder:&nbsp; since when does a brainiac scientist like Reed also have time to be one of the world's greatest experts on judo?&nbsp; <i>Really? </i>(It's a stretch, even for Reed.)\n\nIn the end though, Doom refuses the \"humiliation of being captured by the likes of <i>you</i>!\" and jumps out of the flying ship.\n[ASSET: center | DoomGoneAgain | width=67%]\n\nAnd once again, Doom is gone, and at least for now the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved].\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nA very disjointed adventure, one that was admittedly better than <i>last</i> month's, but never approaching the grandeur of one like [LINK:1962:FF_6|FF #6].&nbsp; It's the kind of issue that feels like it exists mainly to keep Doom in circulation rather than to tell a story worth remembering.\n\nThe story gets point reductions for the endless barrage of whipped-up gadgets which seem to conveniently pop up whenever the plot demands.&nbsp; An early example is Reed's page 3 radar that detects human flesh covered by steel.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=How, pray tell, does it detect Dr. Doom and not say, Iron Man?]\n\nBut that wasn't the only ridiculous thing.&nbsp; To name some others, there was Doom's grappler ray that snatched Alicia off the street and the one-man plastic bubble - the gadgets pile up so quickly that they begin to blur together!&nbsp; Reed even comes up with a ship with a giant magnet in front to cling to the outside of the floating lab!\n\nAlso, Doom has now been featured in 5 of the 17 issues of the FF thus far (almost 30%), and each time has avoided capture or seemingly died.&nbsp; His characterization so far has been quite unsteady as well.\n\nEven at this early stage, Dr. Doom is obviously a fan-favorite, one who keeps returning from one apparent \"final\" appearance to another.&nbsp; But when one actually pauses to consider his string of appearances so far, they seem very haphazard.&nbsp; The imposing villain of issues 5 and 6 feels diminished here, his schemes drifting toward the petty.&nbsp; He infiltrates the FF in issue 10, intending to destroy them from within their ranks.&nbsp; <i>True</i>, that issue had its own problems, but at least it was still <i>some</i> type of plan in action.\n\nSince then, however, it's been less defined.&nbsp; Last month, we learned about his takeover of the micro-world, and his scheme to seize control over the realm, terrorize its citizens and get back at the Fantastic Four, even planning to sell them to aliens into slavery.&nbsp; The issue even depicted him as being upset by the \"contented\" population, as if the notion of happy citizens just going about their everyday lives was somehow abhorrent to him.&nbsp; (That's petty <i>enough</i>, in my book!)\n\nAnd when that scheme didn't work out, he escapes at the end of that issue by returning to his normal size, all so he could reappear this month to do nothing more than devise one inane plan after another to either embarrass or harass the FF -- all for <i>what</i>?&nbsp; His \"modest\" demand for \"a post in the President's cabinet?\"&nbsp; <i>Why?</i>  Does anyone actually believe that Doom would be content to play second fiddle to the President of the United States?&nbsp; (<b><i>I</i></b> sure don't!)\n\nThis issue clearly drifts, meaning that I don't think either Stan or Jack really knew what to do.&nbsp; I think Stan knew where he wanted Doom to <i>end up</i> (to either die or get away yet again), but after reading this, I'm forced now to wonder if that was perhaps the <i>only</i> plot point he discussed with Jack, relying on Kirby to fill in the middle pages.\n\nThe book seems to be in <i>no hurry</i> at all to get going, and even when it finally did, it was just \"Doom terrorizes the nation to get what he wants\" (without any real reasoning behind <i>why</i> he wants it - other than because [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Doom is EVIL!\"]), followed by one endless stream of gadgets, all sent to try to ward off the FF until the page count was finally satisfied, and the next-in-a-long-series-of-apparent-death-scenes could be presented.\n\nDoom gets away -- yet again -- and this time, I don't imagine there's a reader left who actually believes this was the end of him.&nbsp; On the contrary, all that's left for them to wonder about is how soon he'll appear again!\n\n<i>Yes</i>, the page count was \"satisfied,\" but that's hardly the feeling the issue leaves behind.\n\nIn truth, I found myself pushing through the final pages more out of obligation than interest, and by the time Doom made his exit, I almost didn't care how it ended.",
  "letters_page": "After receiving a letter from 31 year-old Harry Warren, the editors wonder who their <i>oldest</i> reader is and wonder aloud: <i>Who can top him</i>?\n\nRonn Foss closes his letter by saying:&nbsp; \"I could close by wishing long life to the MARVEL Comics Group, but I'm sure the AMAZING Mr. Lee will see to that!\"&nbsp; (<i>Heh, so true</i>...)",
  "ownership": "As for my own copy, I got one that was in roughly mid-grade condition as part of the 2000 collection for around $31, ultimately selling it on ebay for $395 in Feb, 2025, netting me over $265.",
  "decoder_ring": "The notion that Victor Von Doom is the ruler of a small European country has obviously not yet occurred to Lee and Kirby.&nbsp; This issue very plainly portrays him as just a citizen of the United States (though hardly an <i>ordinary</i> one), due to his \"modest\" terms of seeking out a seat on the President's cabinet in \"the government.\"\n\nWe'll have to watch the coming issues to see exactly when \"Latveria\" enters the scene -- whether it's developed or sprung on us.",
  "highlight": "The Fantastic Four show up ready for adventure, but Doom's floating lab has more lift than the story they're stuck in."
}